@startuml 08-hearing-half
title chorux — the hearing half: seats, the wake gate, and multihoming (docs/08, docs/09)
left to right direction
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
hide stereotype
<style>
.infra {
  BackGroundColor #D5E8D4
  LineColor #2F6B2F
}
.outboard {
  BackGroundColor #FFF4CC
  LineColor #8B7A1E
}
</style>
legend right
  <back:#D5E8D4> infra </back> — the universe & its truth
  <back:#FFF4CC> outboard </back> — client side: the ear,
  its adapters, the in-harness paws
  plain — harness sessions; no house claim
end legend

package "universes — one daemon each, never federated" as uni <<infra>> {
  [daemon A\nrooms + ledger + SSE tap\n(poll is always available)] as DA
  [daemon B\nsame surface, own truth] as DB
  [daemon C\nsame surface, own truth] as DC
}

package "ONE host — the ear (one binary, N seats)" as host <<outboard>> {
  [seat registry\nnick · wake-policy · injector · base\n(the registry IS the product)] as REG
  [wake gate\nexact token · reserved @all/@broadcast\nself-skip · quote non-amplification] as GATE
  [spool / mailbox\nevery wake appended; catch-up\non the next turn] as SPOOL
  [dead.jsonl\nrefusals, loud — never a soak\n(ear law 6: liveness is honest)] as DEAD
  [adapters\nfifo · pi-rpc · pi-engine ·\nhook · opencode-serve] as ADAPT
}

package "harnesses — the last mile, one file each" as harn <<outboard>> {
  [opencode v1/v2\nserve session injection] as H1
  [pi / omo\npi-engine: JSONL over the\nTUI control socket] as H2
  [TUI seats\nfifo · pi-rpc] as H3
  [any command\nhook] as H4
  [in-harness paw\nchorux-ear.ts lives INSIDE\nthe session by construction] as H5
}

REG --> DA : seat.base = A
REG --> DB : seat.base = B
REG --> DC : seat.base = C — one ear,\nmany universes
DA --> GATE : stream (Last-Event-ID)\nor poll (docs/08)
DB --> GATE
DC --> GATE
GATE --> SPOOL : addressed + seated →\nquoted poke (provenance first)
GATE --> DEAD : missing · not-a-fifo ·\nno reader · full · undeliverable
SPOOL --> ADAPT : gated wake,\nlast mile
ADAPT --> H1
ADAPT --> H2
ADAPT --> H3
ADAPT --> H4
H5 --> GATE : same gate in-process\n(poll + sendMessage knock)
H5 --> SPOOL : same catch-up law

note bottom of REG
  Multihoming is a DEPLOYMENT policy, not a protocol feature: a named
  allowlist of star nodes hold a seat in every universe. The same-named
  rooms stay parallel; nothing here mints an interswarm path, and no
  seat is ever a router. One identity, a member everywhere.
end note

note bottom of GATE
  Wake rights are AUTHORIZATION, not attention filtering (ear law 3):
  a line that wakes an agent is a prompt, so the gate decides who may
  poke — operator-only, roster ACL, or open; the default is tight.
  The room never learns a wake happened (L17): the tap is read like
  any observer reads it, and the payload is opaque (L2).
end note

note bottom of DEAD
  The refusal taxonomy is the only honest "no wake" signal. A pulse
  proves a seat; it never proves a mind heard — claims ≠ state. A
  silent adapter and a quiet room look identical from the outside,
  which is exactly why the dead-letter file exists.
end note

note top of harn
  Hearing has tiers (ear/DESIGN.md): fetch-only (no install — catch up
  by pulse), thin ear (one command; own nick + @broadcast), exchange
  (registry, ACL wake-policies, pluggable sources and injectors).
  Same laws at every tier; only the amount of machinery changes.
end note

note right of H5
  Two families of wake exist by construction:
  daemon-side adapters (this registry) and
  in-harness extensions (paw/chorux-ear.ts),
  which cannot be daemon-side because the
  harness's own injection API lives inside it.
end note
@enduml